# Copy this file to .env, then replace every blank required value.
#
# API_ADMIN_TOKEN can be supplied by the shell instead of this file. Compose
# passes it to api, worker and web and retains it in container configuration.
# Existing-container restarts do not rotate it. See README section 4.
API_ADMIN_TOKEN=
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
FINGERPRINT_KEY=
AWS_REGION=us-east-1

# Optional: the audit trail's MAC key (32+ random bytes as hex, openssl rand -hex 32).
# Empty derives it from FINGERPRINT_KEY. Set it before the first start if FINGERPRINT_KEY
# also goes to an external vault's hasher, and keep it for the life of the audit trail.
# AUDIT_CHAIN_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional: where the key that seals stored connection credentials comes from.
#
# Leave them all empty and it is derived from FINGERPRINT_KEY, exactly as
# before. Nothing below has to be set for CreMore to work. Pick ONE root.
#
# SEALING_KEY separates the two jobs one key used to do. FINGERPRINT_KEY keys
# stored fingerprints and must never rotate, or every recorded match is
# orphaned; the sealing master must be able to rotate. Set a second 32+ random
# bytes as hex to split them:
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# Rotate by moving the old value into SEALING_KEY_PREVIOUS, which opens stored
# credentials but seals no new one.
#
# SEALING_KMS_KEY_ID + SEALING_KEY_CIPHERTEXT go further: the master lives in
# AWS KMS and this file holds only a ciphertext, useless to anyone who copies it
# without kms:Decrypt on that CMK. Generate one without the plaintext key ever
# existing outside KMS:
#
#   aws kms generate-data-key-without-plaintext \
#     --key-id <cmk> --key-spec AES_256 --query CiphertextBlob --output text
#
# Rotate the same way, into SEALING_KEY_CIPHERTEXT_PREVIOUS (comma separated).
#
# SEALING_KEY_VAULT_PATH is the same idea against a Vault/OpenBao you already
# run: a KV v2 path under SECRET_STORE_MOUNT whose SEALING_KEY_VAULT_FIELD
# ("key" by default) holds those 32+ bytes as hex.
#
#   vault kv put secret/cremore/sealing-key key="$(openssl rand -hex 32)"
#
# Both processes read it, so both need a store token: SEALING_VAULT_TOKEN is
# the API's and only has to read that one path, SECRET_STORE_TOKEN is the
# worker's and also reads the connection references it resolves. Rotate into
# SEALING_KEY_VAULT_PATH_PREVIOUS (comma separated).
#
# Or keep SEALING_KEY in this computer's OS keychain and leave it blank here.
# A container cannot reach the host keychain, so the value is exported into the
# environment for one `docker compose up` instead -- Compose prefers the shell
# environment over this file. README "5. (선택) 봉인 키의 보관 위치 분리하기" has
# the command for macOS, Linux and Windows.
#
# SEALING_PASSPHRASE can avoid writing the phrase to this file, but Docker keeps
# injected environment in its container configuration. It is for no AWS, no
# Vault and no keyring -- a headless server reached over SSH. Leave it out of
# this file; type it instead:
#
#   read -rs -p "CreMore sealing passphrase: " SEALING_PASSPHRASE
#   export SEALING_PASSPHRASE && docker compose up -d && unset SEALING_PASSPHRASE
# Existing-container restarts reuse that environment; recreation needs the same phrase.
#
# Its salt comes from FINGERPRINT_KEY, so the same phrase on another
# installation is a different key. Twelve characters minimum; length is the only
# thing that helps. Rotate into SEALING_PASSPHRASE_PREVIOUS (comma separated).
#
# docs/CREDENTIAL_ROOTS.md explains which to choose.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SEALING_KEY=
SEALING_KEY_PREVIOUS=
SEALING_KMS_KEY_ID=
SEALING_KEY_CIPHERTEXT=
SEALING_KEY_CIPHERTEXT_PREVIOUS=
SEALING_KEY_VAULT_PATH=
SEALING_KEY_VAULT_PATH_PREVIOUS=
# Empty means the field is called "key".
SEALING_KEY_VAULT_FIELD=
SEALING_VAULT_TOKEN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Connecting your AWS login. REQUIRED FOR ANY AWS WORK, not just S3 scans.
#
# This is how your AWS CLI login reaches the containers. Without it an empty
# placeholder is mounted at ~/.aws, the AWS identity cannot be approved, and
# every AWS panel stays locked. A bind mount is Docker runtime configuration,
# so this is the one AWS setting the dashboard cannot do for you.
#
# 1. Create the two token-cache directories FIRST. Compose is told not to create
#    host paths itself (create_host_path: false), because Docker would otherwise
#    create them owned by root on Linux and your AWS CLI could no longer write
#    its own token there. They do not exist until your first `aws sso login`.
#    Missing, they behave differently per platform -- a Linux daemon refuses to
#    start the container ("bind source path does not exist") while Docker Desktop
#    creates them for you -- so making them yourself is the one step that works
#    the same everywhere.
#
#      mkdir -p ~/.aws/sso/cache ~/.aws/login/cache          # macOS, Linux
#      mkdir "$env:USERPROFILE\.aws\sso\cache", "$env:USERPROFILE\.aws\login\cache" -Force   # PowerShell
#
# 2. Point AWS_CONFIG_DIR at that directory, written the way YOUR system spells
#    it. Docker needs an absolute path; ~ and %USERPROFILE% are not expanded.
#
#      Windows : AWS_CONFIG_DIR=C:/Users/<you>/.aws        (forward slashes)
#      macOS   : AWS_CONFIG_DIR=/Users/<you>/.aws
#      Linux   : AWS_CONFIG_DIR=/home/<you>/.aws
#
# 3. AWS_PROFILE picks which profile in ~/.aws/config CreMore runs as. Leave it
#    empty to use [default]. CreMore never asks for an access key: it reads
#    whatever this profile resolves to and asks you to approve that identity.
#
# 4. macOS and Linux only: also set CREMORE_UID/CREMORE_GID at the bottom of
#    this file, or the containers run as uid 65532 and cannot read your ~/.aws.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS_CONFIG_DIR=
# AWS_PROFILE=
# Optional: S3 URL of member-account-role.yaml adds an AWS Console Quick Create link to onboarding.
# AWS_ONBOARDING_TEMPLATE_URL=
# Optional: a separate AWS CLI profile for approved key actions (recommended; same sso-session as AWS_PROFILE).
# AWS_REMEDIATION_PROFILE=
# The read-only AWS investigations (privilege simulation, observed use, Secrets Manager
# metadata) are switched on in the dashboard, so nothing here is required. These only
# hard-lock one off for the whole installation:
# IAM_POTENTIAL_ACCESS_MODE=disabled
# AWS_USAGE_LOOKUP_MODE=disabled
# AWS_SECRETS_INVENTORY_MODE=disabled
# Auto-register the organization's accounts (read-only), which is also the AWS Control Tower
# path. NOTHING HERE IS REQUIRED: the StackSet's shared ExternalId, the OU scope and the
# sweep switch are all set in the dashboard (AWS account roles -> Connect AWS account ->
# AWS Organizations), so an operator who cannot edit this file can still onboard an estate.
# Hard-lock the route off for this installation (no dashboard switch can undo it):
# AWS_ORG_DISCOVERY_MODE=disabled
# AWS_ORG_DISCOVERY_INTERVAL_MINUTES=60
# Optional pins: setting one fixes that field so the dashboard cannot change it.
# AWS_ORG_DISCOVERY_EXTERNAL_ID=
# AWS_ORG_DISCOVERY_OU_IDS=ou-abcd-11111111,ou-abcd-22222222
# AWS_ORG_DISCOVERY_EXCLUDE_ACCOUNTS=111111111111,222222222222
# Reach accounts through your own IAM Identity Center assignments instead of a member role
# -- no CloudFormation in any account. NOTHING HERE IS REQUIRED: the permission set and the
# background sweep are both chosen in the dashboard (AWS account roles -> Connect AWS account
# -> IAM Identity Center), so an operator who cannot edit this file can still use the route.
# It uses the `aws sso login` token cached in ~/.aws. The hourly token is renewed with nobody
# present, but only into a WRITABLE cache and only until the client registration expires
# (about 90 days), after which someone must sign in again. deploy/helm ships this off.
# Accounts registered this way can be promoted to a member role later from their card,
# keeping their findings and history.
# Hard-lock the route off for this installation (no dashboard switch can undo it):
# AWS_SSO_DISCOVERY_MODE=disabled
# Or pin the permission set in configuration, which then cannot be changed from the dashboard:
# AWS_SSO_DISCOVERY_PERMISSION_SET=CreMoreScanner
# AWS_SSO_DISCOVERY_INTERVAL_MINUTES=60
# AWS_SSO_DISCOVERY_EXCLUDE_ACCOUNTS=111111111111,222222222222
# Capability probe and cross-host verification are dashboard switches (default
# off). These env values ALLOW them (default) or hard-lock them off:
# CREDENTIAL_CAPABILITY_PROBE_MODE=disabled
# CREDENTIAL_CROSS_HOST_VERIFICATION=disabled
# GitHub Enterprise Server: register each connection's host in the dashboard, and
# list every GHES host here so verification/probing of a discovered token can be
# retried across them. Cross-host retry sends the token to an extra server (off by default).
# GITHUB_ENTERPRISE_HOSTS=ghe.company.com
# CREDENTIAL_CROSS_HOST_VERIFICATION=enabled
# Company domains (Integrations > Other settings > Domain verification) are verified by a DNS TXT record asked of
# this computer's own resolver. To ask other DNS servers instead, list their IP addresses (never host names):
# DOMAIN_DNS_SERVERS=10.0.0.53,10.0.1.53
# Instance metadata lookups are skipped on a PC; set false only when running on EC2 with an instance role.
# AWS_EC2_METADATA_DISABLED=true
# macOS/Linux: run the containers as your own user so they can read ~/.aws.
# Without these the containers run as uid 65532, cannot read your AWS CLI files,
# and the dashboard reports that it has no AWS sign-in. Windows Docker Desktop
# needs neither. Fill them with: id -u  and  id -g
# CREMORE_UID=
# CREMORE_GID=
# Optional: extra dashboard hostnames besides localhost/127.0.0.1/[::1] (comma-separated).
# CREMORE_ALLOWED_HOSTS=
# Optional: scans the worker runs at once, one per task-runner process (1-32). 0 (the default)
# picks min(4, the CPUs Docker gives the container). Each runner holds its own database
# connections and up to about 0.9 GiB of memory while it scans, so lower it on a small PC.
# Schedules, change feeds and queues are read while scans run either way.
# WORKER_CONCURRENCY=0

# Optional GCP worker ADC mount, with deploy/compose/gcp.yml.
# Set an absolute path on the Docker host; no credential JSON belongs in Git.
# GCP_ADC_FILE=/absolute/path/to/application_default_credentials.json
# GCP_QUOTA_PROJECT=your-project-id

# Optional Google account onboarding in the dashboard (API server only).
# An admin can instead save the client in the connection wizard (the secret is sealed);
# any of these settings pins the client and the dashboard cannot change it.
# Create a Web application OAuth client; register the exact callback below.
# Never prefix these with NEXT_PUBLIC_ or commit a filled-in secret.
# GCP_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=your-client.apps.googleusercontent.com
# GCP_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=your-client-secret
# GCP_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/gcp/oauth/callback
# Production: https://your-dashboard.example/gcp/oauth/callback
# See docs/integration/GCP_DASHBOARD_OAUTH_KO.md.
# Optional Roles Anywhere deployment overlay (protected paths, no credential values).
# AWS_ROLES_ANYWHERE_DIRECTORY=/srv/cremore/roles-anywhere
# AWS_SIGNING_HELPER_PATH=/srv/cremore/bin/aws_signing_helper

# Optional: installed ticket-system plugins an admin may choose for owner approvals and
# exposure responses (comma-separated ids). Empty offers none. The Python image includes the
# Jira plugin (packages/cremore-ticket-jira); see docs/TICKET_SYSTEMS.md.
# TICKET_SYSTEM_PLUGINS=jira

# Opt-in process isolation requires the standalone Compose or Helm configuration,
# separate DB roles, offline credential migration and external network enforcement.
# Do not enable by mixing the following variables into the legacy shared Compose.
# See docs/POST_COMPROMISE_CONTAINMENT.md and deploy/compose/isolated/.env.example.
# ISOLATED_DEPLOYMENT=true
# WORKER_EXECUTION_ROLE=collector  # separate processes: collector / remediator / control
# SEALING_PUBLIC_KEYS_FILE=/run/cremore/recipients.json
# SEALING_PRIVATE_KEYS_FILE=/run/cremore/role-private.json  # connector worker only
# CONTROL_SEALING_KEY=  # independently generated; API/control only
# REMEDIATION_APPROVAL_PUBLIC_KEY_FILE=/run/cremore/approval-public.pem
# Signing private key stays OFF application hosts.
